  "problem": "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $C?$\n\nI. $B∨(B∧C)$\n\nII. $(B∧C)∨C$\n\nIII. $(C∧B)∧C$",
  "worked_solution": "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nC∨(C∧B)≡C.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is *not* logically equivalent to $C.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B∨(B∧C) & ≡B≢C.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $C.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(B∧C)∨C & ≡C∨(B∧C) \\\\  & ≡C∨(C∧B) \\\\  & ≡C.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $C.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
